For each time $t$, define $\Lu(t)=\lambda_{Q(t)}$ to be the smallest dyadic scale $\lambda_q$ such that the following two conditions hold:
\begin{align}
\lambda_p^{-1}\norm{u_p(t)}_{L^\infty} &\le c\nu \quad \text{for all } p>q, \label{Hinf}\\
\lambda_q^{-1}\norm{u_{\le q}(t)}_{L^\infty} &\le c\nu. \label{Linf}
\end{align}
Here $c>0$ is a fixed universal constant.

If $t\in B_K$ because condition \eqref{Linf} fails at level $K$, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\norm{u_{\le K}(t)}_{L^\infty} \ge c\nu\lambda_K,
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
\norm{\nabla u(t)}_{L^2}^2 \ge C\nu^2 \lambda_K^{1/2}.
\end{equation}

