statement:
If $t\in B_K$ because condition \eqref{Hinf} fails at some shell $p>K$, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\norm{u_p(t)}_{L^\infty} \ge c\nu\lambda_p,
\end{equation}
then low-mode badness holds at scale $p$ and consequently
\begin{equation}
\norm{\nabla u(t)}_{L^2}^2 \ge C\nu^2 \lambda_p^{1/2} \ge C\nu^2 \lambda_K^{1/2}.
\end{equation}

There exists $C>0$ such that for all $K$ and all $t\in B_K$,
\begin{equation}
\norm{\nabla u(t)}_{L^2}^2 \ge C\nu^2 \lambda_K^{1/2}.
\end{equation}

For every finite $T>0$,
\begin{equation}
|B_K\cap[0,T]|
\le
\frac{C}{\nu^2\lambda_K^{1/2}}
\int_0^T \norm{\nabla u(t)}_{L^2}^2\,dt.
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}
\lim_{K\to\infty} |B_K\cap[0,T]| = 0.
\end{equation}

following_proof:
By the previous proposition,
\begin{equation}
\mathbf 1_{B_K}(t) \le \frac{1}{C\nu^2\lambda_K^{1/2}}\norm{\nabla u(t)}_{L^2}^2.
\end{equation}
Integrating in time and using the Leray--Hopf energy inequality,
$\int_0^T \norm{\nabla u(t)}_{L^2}^2\,dt<\infty$, yields the result.

statement:
\label{thm:plaq_bound}
Let $S^{(k)} = (B^{(k)})^\top B^{(k)}$ be the $k$-th iterated Schur 
complement with effective incidence $B^{(k)}$.  Then the hat matrix 
