For $f = \sum_{n=1}^N a_n\,e^{i n x}$, let $\mathbf{a} = (a_1,\ldots,a_N)^T$
and define the diagonal unitary $D(x) = \mathrm{diag}(e^{ix}, e^{2ix}, \ldots, e^{iNx})$.
Then:
\begin{align*}
  \widetilde{\Gamma}_\gamma(f,f)(x)
  &= \sum_{n,m=1}^N a_n\,\overline{a}_m\,(R_H)_{nm}\,e^{i(n-m)x}
  = \mathbf{b}(x)^*\,R_H\,\mathbf{b}(x), \\
  \widetilde{\Gamma}_{\gamma,2}(f,f)(x)
  &= \sum_{n,m=1}^N a_n\,\overline{a}_m\,(R_H^{\circ 2})_{nm}\,e^{i(n-m)x}
  = \mathbf{b}(x)^*\,R_H^{\circ 2}\,\mathbf{b}(x),
\end{align*}
where $\mathbf{b}(x) = D(x)\,\mathbf{a}$.

As $x$ varies over $\T$, $\mathbf{b}(x)$ traces out an orbit of
$\mathbf{a}$ under unitary conjugation by $D(x)$. Since $D(x)$ is
unitary, the ratio
\[
  \frac{\mathbf{b}(x)^*\,R_H^{\circ 2}\,\mathbf{b}(x)}
       {\mathbf{b}(x)^*\,R_H\,\mathbf{b}(x)}
\]
achieves its infimum over $x$ and $\mathbf{a}$ at the same value as
the infimum over all nonzero vectors $\mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{C}^N$:
\[
  \inf_{\mathbf{c} \neq 0}
  \frac{\mathbf{c}^*\,R_H^{\circ 2}\,\mathbf{c}}
       {\mathbf{c}^*\,R_H\,\mathbf{c}}
  = \lambda_{\min}(R_H^{\circ 2}, R_H).
\]
Here we use that $\{D(x)\,\mathbf{a} : x \in \T,\, \mathbf{a} \in \mathbb{C}^N\}
= \mathbb{C}^N$, since for any target $\mathbf{c}$, we can choose
$a_n = c_n\,e^{-inx}$ for any fixed $x$.

