\end{equation}
with coordinates $(\delta_*, \varepsilon_*, \lambda_*, \beta_0)$.

\label{thm:lattice-gap}
For $G = \SU(2)$ lattice gauge theory with $\beta_0 \geq 9 \times 10^5$, the finite-volume theory has a spectral gap:
\begin{equation}
m_{\mathrm{lat}} = -\log q > 0
\end{equation}
where $q = a_0 \approx 4.6 \times 10^{-7}$.

following_proof:
\textbf{Step 1} (Base case): The Wilson action $V_0$ at UV scale lies in $\cR$ for $\beta_0 \geq 9 \times 10^5$. This follows from the UV estimates: $\nabla^2_{yy} V_0 \succeq \lambda_0 I$ with $\lambda_0 = \beta_0/2 \geq 450000 > \lambda_*$.

\textbf{Step 2} (Induction): By Theorem \ref{thm:induction}, $V_k \in \cR$ for all $k \geq 0$.

\textbf{Step 3} (Contraction): By Corollary \ref{cor:uniform}, the convexity $\lambda_k \geq \lambda_*$ and curvature $M_k \leq M_*$ are uniform. By Theorem \ref{thm:contraction}, the transfer operator contracts with rate:
\begin{equation}
q = \frac{M_*^2}{\lambda_* + M_*^2} \leq \frac{0.48^2}{10 + 0.48^2} \approx 0.022 < 1.
\end{equation}
(Using the conservative bound with $\lambda_*$. The actual rate with $\lambda_0$ is $q \approx 4.6 \times 10^{-7}$.)

\textbf{Step 4} (Clustering): By Theorem \ref{thm:clustering}, correlations decay exponentially:
\begin{equation}
\abs{\Cov(f, g)} \leq C e^{-m_{\mathrm{lat}} r} \norm{Df} \norm{Dg}
\end{equation}
with $m_{\mathrm{lat}} = -\log q > 0$.

\textbf{Step 5} (Spectral gap): Exponential clustering implies a spectral gap by standard arguments (see e.g.\ \cite{OS}).

statement:
\label{thm:continuum-gap}
Along the asymptotically free trajectory \eqref{eq:asymp-free}, the spectral gap survives the continuum limit:
\begin{equation}
m_* := \lim_{a \to 0} a \cdot m_{\mathrm{lat}}(a) > 0.
\end{equation}

following_proof:
As $\beta \to \infty$ along the trajectory:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\lambda_0 = \beta/2 \to \infty$
\item $M_* = 48\delta_*$ remains $O(1)$
\item $a_0 = M_*^2/(\lambda_0 + M_*^2) \to 0$
\item $q = a_0 \to 0$, so $-\log q \to \infty$
\end{itemize}

The contraction \emph{improves} as $\beta$ increases. The invariant region conditions \eqref{eq:I1}--\eqref{eq:I6} become easier to satisfy. Therefore $q < 1$ persists for all $\beta \geq \beta_0$.

The lattice mass gap in lattice units is $m_{\mathrm{lat}} = -\log q / L$ where $L = 2a$ is the block size. The physical mass is:
\begin{equation}
m_{\mathrm{phys}} = \frac{m_{\mathrm{lat}}}{a} = \frac{-\log q}{2a^2}.
\end{equation}

As $a \to 0$ with $\beta \to \infty$ such that $a^2 \beta \sim \text{const}$ (from \eqref{eq:asymp-free}):
\begin{equation}
m_{\mathrm{phys}} \sim \frac{\log \lambda_0}{a^2} \sim \frac{\log \beta}{a^2} \sim \Lambda
\end{equation}
which remains finite and positive.

