\label{prop:helicity}
For solutions of Navier--Stokes:
\begin{equation}
\frac{dH}{dt} = \frac{d(A-B)}{dt} = -2\nu(D_A - D_B).
\end{equation}
Equivalently:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:N-constraint}
\cN^+ - \cN^- = \nu(D_A - D_B).
\end{equation}

following_proof:
The helicity $H = A - B$ evolves as:
\begin{equation}
\frac{dH}{dt} = \frac{dA}{dt} - \frac{dB}{dt} = -2\nu(D_A - D_B) + 2(\cN^+ - \cN^-).
\end{equation}

For the inviscid Euler equations ($\nu = 0$), helicity is conserved: $dH/dt = 0$, hence $\cN^+ = \cN^-$.

For Navier--Stokes, the helicity evolution is purely dissipative:
\begin{equation}
\frac{dH}{dt} = -2\nu(D_A - D_B).
\end{equation}

Comparing with the energy equations yields \eqref{eq:N-constraint}.

