following_proof:
By the operator factorization (Theorem~\ref{thm:factorization}):
\begin{equation}
F - \E[F] = \delta_Z(\Pi_\cA D_Z F).
\end{equation}
Since $\HH_Z = \HH^{1,0} \oplus \HH^{0,1}$:
\begin{align}
\Pi_\cA D_Z F &= \Pi^{1,0} \Pi_\cA D_Z F + \Pi^{0,1} \Pi_\cA D_Z F.
\end{align}
Applying $\delta_Z$ (which is linear by Tier 1):
\begin{equation}
F - \E[F] = \delta_Z(\Pi^{1,0} \Pi_\cA D_Z F) + \delta_Z(\Pi^{0,1} \Pi_\cA D_Z F) = \mathcal{C}[F] + \overline{\mathcal{C}}[F]. \qedhere
\end{equation}

Let $u := \Pi^{1,0} \Pi_\cA D_Z F$ and $v := \Pi^{0,1} \Pi_\cA D_Z F$. Both are adapted (by construction of $\Pi_\cA$).

By Tier 2 (isometry for adapted integrands):
\begin{equation}
\E[\mathcal{C}[F] \cdot \overline{\overline{\mathcal{C}}[F]}] = \E[\delta_Z(u) \cdot \overline{\delta_Z(v)}] = \E[\inner{u}{v}_{\HH_Z}].
\end{equation}

Since $u \in \HH^{1,0}$ and $v \in \HH^{0,1}$, Lemma~\ref{lem:orthogonal} gives $\inner{u}{v}_{\HH_Z} = 0$.

