\label{rem:cyl_meaning}
The closure argument in step (c) is standard functional analysis. The content of the theorem is not the closure but the \emph{criterion}: it reduces the process-dependent question ``does $D_X$ satisfy Leibniz?'' to the structural question ``does $(\delta_X, \HX)$ admit a cylindrical structure?'' This is a nontrivial condition. Gaussian processes satisfy it via Cameron--Martin quasi-invariance (Theorem~\ref{thm:leib_gauss}). Discrete innovation processes satisfy it via algebraic structure (Theorem~\ref{thm:leib_disc}). Compensated Poisson processes satisfy it with a modified Leibniz (Proposition~\ref{prop:levy}). Stochastic volatility martingales satisfy it only with random representers (Theorem~\ref{thm:leib_sv}), and the obstruction (Theorem~\ref{thm:obstr}) proves deterministic representers are impossible. The theorem organizes this classification; it does not merely restate it.

Cylindrical reduction is sufficient for Leibniz, not necessary. Whether a converse holds---whether every process satisfying Leibniz admits some cylindrical structure---is an open question. The known examples suggest this may be true, but we do not prove it.

