For $\beta$ satisfying $\mu_{\mathrm{cs}}\cdot\alpha_{1/2}(\beta)<1$ (i.e., $\beta\lesssim 2.3$ with $\mu_{\mathrm{cs}}\le 1.72$), the mass gap is positive:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mass_bound}
m(\beta) \;\ge\; 4\,m_0(\beta) - \log\bigl(1+3\,\alpha^4\bigr) - 4\log(1+\delta),
\end{equation}
where $m_0=-\log\alpha_{1/2}$, the second term accounts for tube bending (at most 3~directions per step, each costing 4~plaquettes), and $\delta$ is the decoration weight.

following_proof:
The connected correlator satisfies~\eqref{eq:correlator_bound}. Summing over backbones (tubes of length $L\ge d$ with bends) and decorations:
\begin{equation}
\langle O\,O\rangle_c \;\le\; 4\,\bigl[\alpha^4\,(1+3\alpha^4)\,(1+\delta)^4\bigr]^d.
\end{equation}
The tube bending entropy $(1+3\alpha^4)^d$ arises from at most 3~direction changes per step, each adding $4$~plaquettes. The decoration factor $(1+\delta)^{4d}$ uses the hard-core upper bound on the decoration partition function. The sum converges (and $\delta<\infty$) when $\mu_{\mathrm{cs}}\cdot\alpha<1$.\qed

