Equivalently, the limiting Schwinger functions satisfy the continuum Ward identities characteristic of YM in the gauge-invariant sector.

\label{lem:wardlimit}
Assume the lattice Ward identities hold at each $a$ and that the family of observables used to test them is uniformly integrable under the exponential-clustering bounds of Assumption~\ref{assump:clustering}.
Then any subsequential limit $S_n^{(1)}$ satisfies the corresponding continuum Ward identity.

Theorem~\ref{thm:identification} is deliberately structured so that \emph{only} two inputs remain to be proved beyond what is already in hand:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item (Trajectory) a rigorous construction of the UV scaling trajectory in the pure gauge sector showing that tuning the bare coupling $\beta(a)$ suffices and that the induced renormalized coupling obeys asymptotic freedom;
\item (Ward stability) a clean passage from lattice gauge invariance to continuum Ward/BRST identities for the limiting Schwinger functions.
\end{itemize}
Both are standard in philosophy but technically demanding. The point is that the rest of the Clay package (existence, mass gap, nontriviality) is now reduced to these two explicit obligations.

